      "description": "nvm is a per-user, per-shell manager for multiple Node.js versions, written as a POSIX-compliant shell script. It installs, switches and aliases Node releases and reads a project's .nvmrc to pick the right one.",
      "why_interesting": "First time on our list. Version 0.41.0 (4 November) lets you ==select versions by category such as lts, non-lts or alpha==, excludes alphas by default and records each version's LTS status, useful as agent CLIs such as Qwen Code now ask for Node.js 22 or newer.",
      "caveat": "It works in POSIX shells on Unix, macOS and WSL; the README says ==native Windows is not supported== beyond Git Bash or Cygwin in some cases. It loads from your shell startup files, installing means running a remote script, and where nodejs.org has no binary it compiles Node from source, which is slow and needs a C++ toolchain."
    },
